  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nActive Weather and Winds Thursday...\nLingering moisture and upper support continue to produce light\ndrizzle and rain across portions of central and north-central\nWisconsin, but areas further east will likely remain dry for much\nof the early overnight hours. Additional scattered shower coverage\nwill then return to the region in the overnight hours as the deep\nupper trough and attendant cold front cross the region again\nThursday morning. Moisture will be relatively limited with these\nshowers however, so additional rainfall totals will be limited to\naround 40-50% chance of exceeding a quarter of an inch. After the\ninitial push of showers along the cold front, dry conditions are\nlikely to return everywhere but far northern Wisconsin along the\nUpper Peninsula and possibly along the lakeshore. As temperatures\ndrop behind the front overnight, some flurries could mix in at\ntimes and produce a dusting of snow for the northern most portions\nof north-central Wisconsin.\n\nThe cold air advection will also bring some gusty winds to the\nregion today, with widespread gusts to around 20 to 30 mph. Some\ngusts may reach up to 35 mph, but probability of seeing stronger\ngusts has come down, with around only a 20% chance of getting to\naround 40 mph. These stronger winds will likely stick around\nthrough Thursday night into Friday, especially for areas near the\nBay of Green Bay and Lake Michigan.\n\nAfter the initial rounds of precipitation at the beginning of the\nforecast period, dry conditions are largely anticipated for the\nrest of the period.\n\nTemperatures...\nThe cold front will produce a period of near or just below normal\ntemperatures for the overnight period Thursday night and also on\nFriday. A slow warmup then arrives over the weekend into next\nweek, which will bring temperatures back to above normal.",
